{% sec 1, "What Is a Market?", "What is a market, in everyday words?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A {% gl "market" %}market{% endgl %} is any place where people who want to buy something meet people who want to sell it, and they agree a price.</p>
    <p>You've used lots of them. A car boot sale is a market: someone has an old bike, someone else wants one, they haggle and settle on \(\text{£}40\). eBay is a market too, just bigger and online. The London Stock Exchange is a market. So is the giant network of banks swapping pounds, dollars and naira all day and all night. What's being bought changes, the size changes and the speed changes. The idea doesn't.</p>
    <p>Every market has the same two sides:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Buyers</strong> want the thing and have money. Each one has a <em>most</em> they're willing to pay.</li>
      <li><strong>Sellers</strong> have the thing and want money. Each one has a <em>least</em> they're willing to accept.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A trade happens when a buyer's "most" is at or above a seller's "least". Everything else in this note comes from that one sentence.</p>
    <h3>Why both sides walk away happier</h3>
    <p>The same thing is worth different amounts to different people. Someone who has most of their savings in one company's shares values one more share less than a pension fund that owns a bit of everything. A farmer cares more about knowing next year's grain price than a food company does. That difference is why trades happen at all.</p>
    <p>Economists call your "most" or "least" your {% gl "reservation price" %}reservation price{% endgl %}. Say a holder would sell a share for anything above \(\text{£}98\), and an investor would pay up to \(\text{£}103\). Any price between the two works for both of them. If they settle at \(\text{£}100\), the seller is \(\text{£}2\) better off than their minimum and the buyer is \(\text{£}3\) better off than their maximum. Between them, the trade has created \(\text{£}5\) of value out of thin air. That gain is called the {% gl "surplus" %}surplus{% endgl %}.</p>
    <p>The price doesn't change how much surplus there is. It only decides who gets which share of it. What eats into it is the cost of trading: fees, the gap between buying and selling prices, and the price moving against you as you trade. A good market is one that lets as many of these trades happen as possible, with as little of the surplus lost along the way.</p>
    <h3>Who meets who</h3>
    <p>The biggest markets exist because two groups need each other. People, pension funds and insurers have spare money they want to grow. Companies and governments need money to build factories, roads and schools. The market is where they meet.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Who meets who</h3><p>Money flows one way. Proof that you own a piece of something, or that you're owed your money back, flows the other.</p></div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-meet" viewBox="0 0 640 220" role="img" aria-label="Savers and investors meet firms and governments in the market. Money flows to the firms, and shares and bonds flow back to the savers."></svg>
    <p class="look"><strong>Things to notice.</strong> The shares and bonds coming back are exactly the things that get traded afterwards. They're the first two asset classes, and the next note tours all of them.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>A market is wherever buyers and sellers meet and agree a price. Nobody hands the price down. It comes out of everyone's wants meeting.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "Four Jobs a Market Does", "Why do we need markets at all?", "Four jobs" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Markets aren't just places to swap things. They do four jobs that the rest of the economy relies on.</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>It finds prices.<span class="why">Thousands of people each bring what they know and what they want. The price is the one number that sums all of that up. Nobody has to work it out on their own. This job is called {% gl "price discovery" %}price discovery{% endgl %}, and section 3 shows how it works.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>It lets companies and governments raise money.<span class="why">A company sells new shares to get money to grow. A government sells bonds to borrow for roads and hospitals. That first sale is how money gets from savers to the people who need it. It's the subject of the "first sale vs resale" note in this chapter.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>It lets people pass risk on.<span class="why">An airline doesn't know what fuel will cost next year, so it can agree a price today with someone happy to take that risk. An importer paying a supplier in dollars can fix its exchange rate in advance. The risk doesn't vanish. It moves to someone who wants it, for a price. That's what derivatives are for.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>It lets you get out.<span class="why">Because there's always someone there, you can turn what you own back into cash when you need to. Selling a share takes seconds. Selling a house can take months. How easily you can get in and out without moving the price is called {% gl "liquidity" %}liquidity{% endgl %}.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Why everyone meets in one place</h3>
    <p>Imagine there was no market, and every buyer had to find a seller on their own. You'd ring round, haggle with each person, have no idea what anyone else was paying, and just hope the other side actually paid up. With a handful of people that's annoying. With thousands it's impossible, because the number of possible pairs explodes.</p>
    <p>Put everyone in one meeting place and the problem shrinks: each person needs one connection, to the market, instead of one to everybody else. There's a bonus too. When every trade happens in one place, everyone can see the prices people are actually paying. The market gives everyone a public price for free, just by existing.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Everyone for themselves vs one meeting place</h3><p>Add people and count the connections each way.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="ctrl"><label for="net-n">People</label><input type="range" id="net-n" min="3" max="40" step="1" value="8"><output id="net-n-out" for="net-n">8</output></div>
    </div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-net" viewBox="0 0 640 270" role="img" aria-label="Left: everyone connected to everyone else. Right: everyone connected to one central market."></svg>
    <div class="readout" id="net-read"></div>
    <p class="look"><strong>Things to notice.</strong> Double the people and the tangle on the left roughly quadruples, while the right only doubles. At \(100\) people it's \(4{,}950\) possible pairs against \(100\) connections. The maths is in section 8.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Liquidity is a service, and someone gets paid for it</h3>
    <p>Even in one meeting place, buyers and sellers rarely turn up at the same moment wanting the same amount. A pension fund wants to sell a million shares at 10am. The buyers who'd take them might not arrive until the afternoon.</p>
    <p>Someone has to bridge that gap in time: buy now from whoever's selling, hold it for a while, and sell later to whoever comes to buy. That's what a {% gl "market maker" %}market maker{% endgl %} does. Holding something you didn't really want is risky, so they're paid for it through the gap between the price they buy at and the price they sell at, the {% gl "bid-ask spread" %}bid-ask spread{% endgl %}. A lot of trading, and the whole Market Making chapter in Quant, is about that business.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "How the Price Is Found", "If nobody sets the price, where does it come from?", "Finding the price" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Take tickets for a big match. Some fans would pay a lot for one, some only a little. Some ticket holders would sell for not much, others would need a big offer before they'd give theirs up.</p>
    <p>Now put everyone on one chart, with the price going up the side and the number of tickets along the bottom.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>buyers line</strong> ({% gl "demand" %}demand{% endgl %}) slopes <em>down</em>. The cheaper the ticket, the more fans are happy to buy one.</li>
      <li>The <strong>sellers line</strong> ({% gl "supply" %}supply{% endgl %}) slopes <em>up</em>. The higher the price, the more holders are tempted to sell.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Where the two lines cross is the {% gl "market price" %}market price{% endgl %}. It's the only price where everyone who wants to trade finds someone to trade with.</p>
    <p><strong>Below the crossing</strong>, more people want to buy than sell. Fans who can't get a ticket start offering more, and the price gets pushed up. <strong>Above the crossing</strong>, more people want to sell than buy. Holders who can't find a buyer start dropping their price, and it gets pushed down. Either way the price is pulled back to the crossing, without anybody being in charge.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Match tickets: move the news</h3><p>Press a piece of news and watch the crossing point move. The dashed lines show where the market was before the news.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="presets" aria-label="News that moves the market">
