    <p class="look" id="sd-story">Pick a piece of news. Nobody sets the price: it sits wherever the two lines cross.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>The price sits where the buyers line and the sellers line cross. If it's anywhere else, someone is left wanting, and they push it back.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 4, "Why Prices Move Every Day", "If the price sits at the crossing, why does it keep changing?", "Why prices move" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The price only moves when one of the lines moves. And the lines move because of news: anything that changes how many people want to buy, or how many want to sell, at every price.</p>
    <p>In real markets that looks like this:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A company announces bigger profits than anyone expected. More people want its shares, so the buyers line shifts right and the share price rises.</li>
      <li>A war threatens the countries that pump oil. Less oil will be for sale, so the sellers line shifts left and the oil price rises.</li>
      <li>People get scared about the economy. They want something that holds its value, so more of them buy gold and the gold price rises.</li>
      <li>A central bank raises interest rates. Holding that country's currency now pays more, so more people want it and it gets stronger.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>How the desk talks about it</h3>
    <p>When lots of people are trying to buy at once, traders say there's {% gl "buying pressure" %}buying pressure{% endgl %}. When lots are trying to sell, there's selling pressure. Same chart: one line has shifted, and the price is on its way to the new crossing.</p>
    <p>Here's the part that surprises people. By the time you read the news on your phone, the price has usually already moved. Thousands of traders saw it first and acted in seconds. Traders say the news is {% gl "priced in" %}priced in{% endgl %}.</p>
    <p>That leads to a strange-sounding rule: <strong>markets move on surprises, not on news.</strong> If everyone expects a company to make \(\text{£}1\) billion and it makes \(\text{£}1\) billion, the share price may hardly move. The lines were already sitting where that result put them. If it makes \(\text{£}1.3\) billion, that's a surprise, and the buyers line jumps.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>Prices move when the buyers line or the sellers line moves. Those lines move on news, but only on the part of the news nobody expected.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 5, "How Good Is the Market at Finding the Price?", "Does the price really capture everything people know?", "How good is the price?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Every trader acts on something: their research, a model, a company they need to protect themselves against, or just cash coming in or going out. When someone who thinks a share is too cheap buys it, their buying nudges the price up, towards what they think it's worth. Everyone watching sees the price move, even though they never see the reason. So the price at any moment isn't anyone's opinion. It's where all that buying and selling currently balances.</p>
    <h3>Why the crowd is often right</h3>
    <p>Ask five analysts what a share is worth and you'll get five answers: say \(\text{£}98\), \(\text{£}101\), \(\text{£}103\), \(\text{£}99\) and \(\text{£}104\). Each one is a bit off, but the ones guessing too high and the ones guessing too low partly cancel out. Their average, \(\text{£}101\), is usually closer to the truth than most of them on their own. A market does something like this all day, with thousands of people.</p>
    <p>It goes wrong in ways you can spot:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Only one side knows.</strong> If a handful of people have information nobody else has, there's nothing for their errors to cancel against.</li>
      <li><strong>Everyone copies everyone.</strong> The cancelling-out only works if people think for themselves. When they follow each other, the mistakes pile up in the same direction. That's how bubbles start.</li>
      <li><strong>Trades that have nothing to do with value.</strong> A fund forced to sell because investors want their money back still pushes the price down, even though nothing about the company changed.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Two ways news gets into the price</h3>
    <p><strong>1. Public news: the price jumps before anyone trades.</strong> When big economic figures come out, everyone sees them at the same instant. The people quoting prices don't wait to be traded with. They just move their prices straight away. In the lesson's example, a US share index future sits at \(5{,}000\) before a data release. The number is better than expected, and within a second the market is quoting \(5{,}010\), with only a few contracts actually traded on the way up. The trading comes afterwards, as people who read the news differently trade at the new level.</p>
    <p><strong>2. Private information: the price drifts as people trade.</strong> Someone who knows more than everyone else has to trade to make money from it. Nobody sees who they are or why they're buying, but everyone sees the buying, and prices adjust. This channel is slower and noisier, and it's the biggest danger for a market maker. Getting traded against by someone who knows more is called {% gl "adverse selection" %}adverse selection{% endgl %}, and it has its own lesson in the Quant topic.</p>
    <h3>So are markets "efficient"?</h3>
    <p>The {% gl "efficient market hypothesis" %}efficient market hypothesis{% endgl %} says prices already reflect what's known. It comes in three strengths:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Version</th><th>What the price already reflects</th><th>So you can't make easy money from…</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Weak</td><td>Every past price</td><td>Patterns in the chart</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Semi-strong</td><td>Everything that's public</td><td>News, accounts and reports</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Strong</td><td>Everything, even private information</td><td>Anything at all, even inside knowledge</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The best way to use it is as a yardstick, not a verdict. Markets are efficient enough that easy money is rare, and inefficient enough that the people who fix wrong prices get paid for doing it. That isn't a contradiction: <strong>prices are good precisely because someone profits from making them good.</strong> It's the same conclusion I came to reading <a href="/blog/a-random-walk-down-wall-street.html">A Random Walk Down Wall Street</a>.</p>
    <p>One more thing that's easy to get backwards: a price jumping a lot after real news is the market <em>working</em>. The warning signs are the opposite: prices moving a lot when there's no news, or news arriving and the price not moving at all, which usually means hardly anyone is trading and the last price is out of date.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>The price is the market's best guess, pieced together from everyone's trades. It's usually good, because people get paid for correcting it, and it goes wrong when information is one-sided or everyone copies each other.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 6, "Key Words", "What do economists and the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Market</h3><span class="tag">Everyday</span></div><p>Anywhere buyers and sellers meet and agree a price. It can be a car boot sale, a website or a global network of banks.</p><p class="eg">The FX market has no building at all: it's banks and brokers trading with each other.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Demand</h3><span class="tag">Economics</span></div><p>How much of something people want to buy at each price. Drawn as the buyers line, sloping down.</p><p class="eg">At \(\text{£}80\) only \(300\) fans want a ticket. At \(\text{£}40\), \(700\) do.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Supply</h3><span class="tag">Economics</span></div><p>How much of something people are willing to sell at each price. Drawn as the sellers line, sloping up.</p><p class="eg">At \(\text{£}40\) only \(300\) holders will sell. At \(\text{£}80\), \(700\) will.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Market price</h3><span class="tag">Economics</span></div><p>The price where the amount people want to buy equals the amount people want to sell. Economists also call it the equilibrium price.</p><p class="eg">In the ticket chart it starts at \(\text{£}60\), with \(500\) tickets changing hands.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Reservation price</h3><span class="tag">Economics</span></div><p>The most a buyer would pay, or the least a seller would accept, before walking away.</p><p class="eg">A holder won't sell below \(\text{£}98\): that's their reservation price.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Surplus</h3><span class="tag">Economics</span></div><p>The value a trade creates: the gap between the buyer's most and the seller's least. The price splits it between them.</p><p class="eg">\(\text{£}103 - \text{£}98 = \text{£}5\) of surplus, whatever price they agree.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Price discovery</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The market finding the price by pulling buyers and sellers together, rather than anyone deciding it.</p><p class="eg">The first trades of the morning "discover" where a share should open after overnight news.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Liquidity</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>How easily you can buy or sell without moving the price much. Lots of buyers and sellers means liquid. Few means illiquid.</p><p class="eg">EUR/USD is very liquid. A house is very illiquid.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Buying and selling pressure</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Lots of people trying to buy (or sell) at once, pushing the price towards a new crossing.</p><p class="eg">"Strong buying pressure in gold after the news."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Priced in</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>News the market already expected, so it's already reflected in the price. Only surprises move it.</p><p class="eg">"The rate cut was fully priced in, so the pound barely moved."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Adverse selection</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Being traded against by someone who knows more than you. The trades you win are the ones you'd rather not have.</p><p class="eg">"Everything we bought this morning went down: classic adverse selection."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Efficient market hypothesis</h3><span class="tag">Economics</span></div><p>The idea that prices already reflect available information, so beating the market consistently is hard. Comes in weak, semi-strong and strong versions.</p><p class="eg">"In a market this efficient, any edge has to come from somewhere others aren't looking."</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 7, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where this chart turns up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: guessing which line moves next</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>FX is this chart running all day and night around the world. The price of the pound in dollars is wherever the people who want pounds meet the people who want to swap them for dollars. If UK interest rates rise, holding pounds pays more, more people want them, the buyers line shifts right, and the pound strengthens.</p>
      <p>Gold works the same way, with one twist: people buy it when they're scared. A crisis shifts the buyers line right even though nothing about gold itself has changed.</p>
      <p>Seen this way, a trader's job is simple to say and hard to do: <strong>work out which line is about to move, and which way, before it's priced in.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: the same chart, much slower</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>House prices are set by the same two lines. Where few homes are for sale and lots of people want to live there, the sellers line sits far left and the buyers line far right, so prices are high. When mortgage rates rise, fewer people can afford to borrow, the buyers line shifts left, and prices cool.</p>
      <p>The big difference is liquidity. A share can be sold in a second. A house can take months, and the price often has to drop to find a buyer quickly. That's why property is called an illiquid asset, and why it matters when planning how long money will be tied up.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: the naira</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The naira's price in dollars is a market too. On one side are people who want naira: exporters turning their dollar earnings into naira, and foreign investors coming in. On the other are people who want dollars: importers paying overseas suppliers, and people saving in dollars to protect themselves. When far more people want dollars than naira, the naira weakens.</p>
      <p>Section 3 also explains what happens when a price is held below the crossing by rule. More people want to buy than can, so there's a shortage. In currency markets that shows up as queues for dollars at the official rate, and a separate street rate where the "real" crossing price gets paid. Fixing that gap, and making it easy for people and businesses to get the currency they need, is a big part of what fintech in Nigeria works on.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 8, "The Maths Underneath", "How do you find the crossing point with algebra?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Both lines in the ticket chart are straight, so each one can be written as an equation. Call the price \(P\) (in pounds) and the number of tickets traded \(Q\).</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">The ticket market</span>\[ \text{Demand: } P = 110 - \frac{Q}{10} \qquad \text{Supply: } P = 10 + \frac{Q}{10} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>What each number means</h3>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>The demand line has a negative gradient, \(-\tfrac{1}{10}\).<span class="why">Every extra \(10\) tickets sold needs the price to drop \(\text{£}1\) to tempt one more batch of fans in.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>The supply line has a positive gradient, \(+\tfrac{1}{10}\).<span class="why">Every extra \(10\) tickets needs the price to rise \(\text{£}1\) to tempt more holders to sell.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>The demand line starts at \(\text{£}110\).<span class="why">That's the most the keenest fan would pay. Above it, nobody buys.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>The supply line starts at \(\text{£}10\).<span class="why">That's the least the keenest seller would accept. Below it, nobody sells.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>At the market price, buyers and sellers agree, so both equations give the same \(P\). That means you can set them equal and solve. It's a pair of simultaneous equations.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 1</span>
    <h3>Find the market price and the number of tickets traded.</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Set the two equations equal: \(110 - \tfrac{Q}{10} = 10 + \tfrac{Q}{10}\).</li>
      <li>Collect the \(Q\) terms: \(100 = \tfrac{2Q}{10}\), so \(Q = 500\).</li>
      <li>Put \(Q = 500\) back into either line: \(P = 10 + \tfrac{500}{10} = \text{£}60\).</li>
      <li>Check with the other line: \(110 - 50 = 60\). ✓</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">\(500\) tickets change hands at \(\text{£}60\). That's the starting point of the chart in section 3.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 2</span>
    <h3>A big match is announced. Every fan will now pay \(\text{£}20\) more than before. What happens?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>The demand line moves up by \(20\): \(P = 130 - \tfrac{Q}{10}\). Supply doesn't change.</li>
      <li>Set equal: \(130 - \tfrac{Q}{10} = 10 + \tfrac{Q}{10}\), so \(120 = \tfrac{2Q}{10}\) and \(Q = 600\).</li>
      <li>\(P = 10 + 60 = \text{£}70\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">The price rises from \(\text{£}60\) to \(\text{£}70\), and \(100\) more tickets change hands. Press "Big match announced" in section 3 and you'll see exactly this.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h3>The general version</h3>
    <p>Any straight-line market can be written with four numbers, and the crossing always comes out the same way:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Market price for straight lines</span>\[ \text{Demand: } P = a - bQ \qquad \text{Supply: } P = c + dQ \qquad \Rightarrow \qquad Q^{*} = \frac{a - c}{b + d}, \quad P^{*} = a - bQ^{*} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The top line, \(a - c\), is the gap between what the keenest buyer would pay and what the keenest seller would accept. The bigger that gap, the more trades happen. If \(a\) is below \(c\), even the keenest buyer won't pay what the keenest seller wants, and nothing trades at all.</p>
    <h3>Surplus: how a trade's value gets split</h3>
    <p>Call the seller's least \(r_s\), the buyer's most \(r_b\) and the price they agree \(P\). A trade only happens if \(r_s \le P \le r_b\).</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Surplus from one trade</span>\[ \text{seller's gain} = P - r_s \qquad \text{buyer's gain} = r_b - P \qquad \text{total} = r_b - r_s \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Add the two gains and \(P\) cancels out. That's the algebra behind "the price only decides who gets which share".</p>
    <h3>Counting the connections</h3>
    <p>With \(n\) people and no market, how many different pairs could trade? Each person could pair with the \(n - 1\) others, which gives \(n(n - 1)\). But that counts every pair twice (Ade with Bola, and Bola with Ade), so halve it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Pairs vs connections to one market</span>\[ \text{pairs} = \frac{n(n-1)}{2} \qquad \text{connections to one market} = n \]</div>
  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 3</span>
    <h3>How many pairs are there with \(100\) people?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>\(\dfrac{100 \times 99}{2} = \dfrac{9{,}900}{2} = 4{,}950\) possible pairs.</li>
      <li>With one market in the middle: \(100\) connections.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Almost \(50\) times fewer. The pairs grow roughly like \(n^2\) (a quadratic), while the connections grow like \(n\) (a straight line). Central markets turn a quadratic problem into a linear one.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Why averaging guesses helps</h3>
    <p>The five analysts' average is \(\dfrac{98 + 101 + 103 + 99 + 104}{5} = \dfrac{505}{5} = \text{£}101\). If each guess is off by a random amount with the same typical size, and the guesses are independent, the average of \(n\) of them is typically off by only \(\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{n}}\) as much. With \(100\) independent guesses the typical error shrinks to a tenth. The catch is the word <em>independent</em>: if everyone copies the same source, the errors don't cancel at all. This idea gets its proper treatment in Probability and Statistics.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>Demand is \(P = 90 - \tfrac{Q}{5}\) and supply is \(P = 20 + \tfrac{Q}{5}\). Find the market price and quantity.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(90 - \tfrac{Q}{5} = 20 + \tfrac{Q}{5}\), so \(70 = \tfrac{2Q}{5}\) and \(Q = 175\).</span><span>\(P = 20 + 35 = \text{£}55\). Check: \(90 - 35 = 55\). ✓</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>In question 1, a rule fixes the price at \(\text{£}40\). How many people want to buy, and how many want to sell?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Buyers: \(40 = 90 - \tfrac{Q}{5}\), so \(Q = 250\). Sellers: \(40 = 20 + \tfrac{Q}{5}\), so \(Q = 100\).</span><span>Only \(100\) trades happen and \(150\) buyers are left with nothing. That's a shortage, the same thing that happens with currency queues at a fixed official rate.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>Oil-producing countries agree to pump less. Which line moves, which way, and what happens to the price?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Less oil is for sale at every price, so the sellers (supply) line shifts left. The crossing moves up: the price rises and less oil is traded.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>A company reports profits exactly in line with what everyone expected. Why might its share price barely move?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>The result was already priced in. Traders had bought and sold based on what they expected, so the lines were already in place. Only a surprise would shift them.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>\(50\) people could each trade with any of the others directly. How many different pairs is that?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(\dfrac{50 \times 49}{2} = \dfrac{2{,}450}{2} = 1{,}225\) pairs, against \(50\) connections to one market.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">6</span><span>A seller's reservation price is \(\text{£}97\) and a buyer's is \(\text{£}105\). They trade at \(\text{£}100\). How much surplus does each side get?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Buyer: \(105 - 100 = \text{£}5\). Seller: \(100 - 97 = \text{£}3\).</span><span>Total: \(105 - 97 = \text{£}8\), whatever the price.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">7</span><span>A share is quoted \(\text{£}50.00\) to buy from you and \(\text{£}50.10\) to sell to you. News comes out and, with almost no trading, the quotes jump to \(\text{£}52.00\) and \(\text{£}52.10\). How far did the middle price move, and what does the lack of trading tell you?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>The middle went from \(\tfrac{50.00 + 50.10}{2} = 50.05\) to \(\tfrac{52.00 + 52.10}{2} = 52.05\): up \(\text{£}2.00\).</span><span>It was public news. The people quoting prices simply moved them, so the price changed before the volume arrived.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}
